      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1113,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **H₂O**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water forms droplets and flows.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance found naturally on Earth in all three physical states:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (frozen water)\n*   **Liquid:** Water (the state we drink and swim in)\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible gas)\n\nThis ability to change states easily is the engine of the **water cycle**, which moves water around the planet.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several physical properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to carry nutrients and minerals through the body and transport chemicals in the environment.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and release a lot of heat before it gets cold. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the human body stable.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of life on Earth. It is essential for:\n*   **Biological Functions:** It acts as a medium for chemical reactions in cells and helps regulate body temperature through sweating and panting.\n*   **Ecosystems:** It supports all known forms of life, from microscopic bacteria to blue whales.\n*   **Geography:** It covers about 71% of the Earth's surface, filling the oceans, rivers, lakes, and ice caps."
